WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
Open a French novel and you'll
read lines like «Il pleuvait.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:07.880
Soudain, elle entra.»
Novels print «il parla»,

3
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.970
he spoke. In conversation,
you'd hear «il a parlé».

4
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.060
Same event, same meaning.

5
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.890
Two different pasts.

6
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.520
Yet Albert Camus told «L'Étranger»,

7
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.380
from 1942, in the everyday tense instead.

8
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.820
Critics often discuss that choice.

9
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.560
So why would novels choose
a past tense that people

10
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.050
almost never say out loud?

11
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.820
In this mission, you'll
play stem detective,

12
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.300
tracing strange verb forms
back to verbs you know.

13
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.430
Then you'll tag every
verb in a short scene:

14
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.230
background or event?

15
00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.230
Your mission: crack the
secret of a past tense

16
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.370
almost nobody says out loud.
